WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.359 --> 00:00:10.949
Hi, welcome back to the Wheel of Life online retreat.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:18.750
I'm happy that Stanley is translating for me again. Thank you.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:25.560
The name of this session is "Dead End Beginning."

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.270
"Dead end" means "blocked way."

00:00:41.469 --> 00:00:45.389
So the picture you see here is an alleyway, a narrow street,

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:53.070
and somebody built a wall there and you can't go any further.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:05.129
This whole class is going to be about "how is it that you take rebirth?"

00:01:08.409 --> 00:01:13.618
And then about halfway through the class, we're going to switch horses and go in a different way.

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:20.250
sumpa kye si du nying tsam jor tsul ni
(GSUM PA SKYE SRID DU NYING MTSAMS SBYOR TSUL NI)

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:23.070
Here's the third

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:28.320
section about "how is it that we have such trouble in this life?"

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.549
nying tsam jor (NYING MTSAMS SBYOR) here is an interesting idiom.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:49.110
It means how do we make the "crossing into a new life," like crossing a river into a new country.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:02.540
ngel kye la tsun na (MNGAL SKYES LA MTSON NA)--
so there are different ways to take birth, like egg,

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.560
warmth and moisture (like mosquitoes),

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.580
miraculous birth (like Padmasambhava),

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:26.489
but we can say standard birth for people like us.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:35.699
It's called ngel kye (MNGAL SKYES). ngel kye means to be "born in a womb" of a woman,

00:02:41.019 --> 00:02:43.109
and here's how it happens.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.190
You can find good descriptions in the Vinaya scriptures,

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:59.729
because they are discussing "when does the person start?",

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:09.480
and Buddhism says that there is a living being at the moment of conception.

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:22.350
pa may ku trak la pa ma ... 
(PHA MA'I KHU KHRAG LA PHA MA ...)

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:25.230
... nelpo chepar tong
(... NYAL PO BYED PAR MTHONG)--

00:03:25.299 --> 00:03:27.299
So it's kind of a hallucination,

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:32.789
and the bardo (BAR DO)--spirit--being,

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:36.930
they enter the womb

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:45.220
and the mother's menstrual blood and the father's semen is there,

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:53.640
and they have this hallucination where they actually see their father and their mother

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:05.500
having sex. And they're like, "Oh, what's that? It looks very, very fun."

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:16.649
And so they come closer and closer, and there's a tradition here that says they're chak dang gi gyuk (CHAGS SDANG GIS RGYUGS). 

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:24.779
gyuk (RGYUGS) means they "hurry" to that place,

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:31.739
and then they have different emotions and

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:36.510
the one I remember my teacher describing

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:43.049
is called chak dang (CHAGS SDANG). chak dang means 

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:46.350
"attracted and repulsed."

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:59.489
chak (CHAGS) means ... if the bardo being, for example, is going to be a male,

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:10.769
then they see sex going on and, you know, like all males, they're like, "Wow, what's that? What's that?"

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.229
Then they see their father having the sex and they feel competition or something.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:33.960
But anyway, they're attracted by the sexual act that they think they see,

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:43.380
then they get stuck in the blood and the semen,

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:50.299
and they get frustrated and upset,

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:59.510
kong tru shi te (KHONG KHROS SHI STE)--they die from their bardo

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:04.760
existence with a feeling of kind of upset and angry.

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:19.519
And then kye di du nying tsam jor--they make the crossing into the new life, in that feeling.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:28.369
de dak gi lam rim dringpo dang, de nyur ...
(DE DAG GIS LAM RIM 'BRING PO DANG, BDE MYUR ...)

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:31.229
... ne jungwa tar ...
(... NAS 'BYUNG BA LTAR ...)

00:06:31.229 --> 00:06:38.389
... kun jung korway juk rim sampa
(... KUN 'BYUNG 'KHOR BA'I 'JUG RIM BSAM PA)
All of this section that I've described to you so far, and in this retreat,

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:52.850
this is the contemplation on the truth of the source of suffering.

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:04.309
We discussed negative emotions in the last retreat.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:10.369
We discussed the karma those negative emotions cause you to make.

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:20.359
And then we've described how that karma forces you into death, into bardo, and into a new life.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:33.169
This whole presentation of how we get in trouble with this life,

00:07:38.699 --> 00:07:42.049
it's based on some famous lam rim (LAM RIM) books.

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:48.830
In general, there are eight famous lam rims.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:56.000
And actually this lam rim, which is a ninth one.

00:07:58.919 --> 00:08:05.478
In this one, Pabongka Rinpoche, in the 1920s when he taught it, he used all eight of those other lam rims.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:18.349
And at this point in his lecture,

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:25.520
he says okay this whole description that we've had in this retreat so far

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:34.880
that's based on the middle length lam rim of Je Tsongkapa,

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:44.929
it's based on the "Path of Bliss" of the First Panchen Lama,

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:58.099
and it's based on the book called "Quick Path," which is by His Holiness the Second Panchen Lama.

00:09:06.329 --> 00:09:09.529
da ni lam rim de nyur gyi sa che du mi sel shing
(DA NI LAM RIM BDE MYUR GYI SA BCAD DU MI GSAL ZHING)

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:12.709
But let's turn to a different

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:17.719
presentation, which you don't really see clearly in those lam rims.

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:34.789
Let's turn to the presentation of this subject of how lousy this life is as it was presented in the great lam rim of Tsongkhapa.

00:09:45.269 --> 00:09:49.818
What outline does Tsongkhapa use in his very, very famous

00:09:50.279 --> 00:09:57.798
"The Great Book on the Steps of the Path" to describe how we get into this trouble, how we get into this life.

00:10:10.949 --> 00:10:14.508
And in Tsongkhapa's "Great Book on the Steps of the Path,"

00:10:18.689 --> 00:10:23.089
he uses the teaching of the twelve links of dependence.

00:10:27.479 --> 00:10:29.009
And

00:10:29.009 --> 00:10:32.719
we can kind of follow another book called jam pel shel lung 
('JAM DPAL ZHAL LUNG),

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:37.339
which is called "The Word of the Gentle One," "The Word of Manjushri."

00:10:45.209 --> 00:10:48.078
This lam rim was written by His Holiness the Fifth Dalai Lama.

00:10:53.159 --> 00:10:55.159
sa che tsam shuwa la
(SA BCAD TZAM ZHU BA LA)--

00:10:57.359 --> 00:11:02.389
I'm just going to give you the outline and we're not going to go into much detail.

00:11:08.369 --> 00:11:10.369
So we're actually going to move now to

00:11:11.069 --> 00:11:16.459
discussing how the trouble starts, but this time we're going to use the Wheel of Life.

00:11:23.099 --> 00:11:25.099
And

00:11:25.289 --> 00:11:30.649
let's go back to the text.  
de tar kun jung korway juk rim sam tsul dupa che na ...
DE LTAR KUN 'BYUNG 'KHOR BA'I 'JUG RIM BSAM TSUL BSDUS PA BYED NA ...)

00:11:31.529 --> 00:11:38.029
ngar shepa de chok mu
(... SNGAR BSHAD PA DES CHOG MOD)--
Look, you can give this explanation of how we get into sansara

00:11:38.029 --> 00:11:41.448
based on that method we used up until now.

00:11:51.209 --> 00:11:54.228
un kyang tsennyi kyi shung la jangpa songwa sok kyi
('ON KYANG MTSAN NYID KYI GZHUNG LA SBYANGS PA SONG BA SOGS KYIS)--

00:11:54.569 --> 00:11:59.298
But I know there's people in the audience today--and same with you--

00:12:06.029 --> 00:12:10.158
who are interested in the great classics of Buddhism.

00:12:14.189 --> 00:12:18.348
And that's kind of the emphasis of our group.

00:12:21.809 --> 00:12:24.108
We like to take the time to go deeper and deeper,

00:12:27.569 --> 00:12:29.569
and we go into the classics.

00:12:30.659 --> 00:12:33.439
And here Pabongka Rinpoche says,

00:12:35.429 --> 00:12:39.228
"If you're that kind of person who wants to go deeper ..."

00:12:44.189 --> 00:12:51.319
gangsak gi kyepar gashik la di lek te
(GANG ZAG GI KHYAD PAR 'GA' ZHIG LA 'DI LEGS TE)--
for those kind of special people, "it would be better to give them the twelve links."

00:12:57.959 --> 00:13:01.608
And then he jumps horses,

00:13:04.079 --> 00:13:09.709
and he starts to teach the whole system of how we get into trouble in this life

00:13:15.029 --> 00:13:18.019
based on this ancient teaching of the Wheel of Life.

00:13:22.229 --> 00:13:26.089
So first he mentions a little bit about the history of the Wheel of Life.

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:36.019
ngun yul u kyi gyel pu (SNGON YUL DBUS KYI RGYAL POS)--
Once upon a time long ago,

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:41.240
the king of central India--

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:49.160
and he doesn't say that king's name, but we know it was a king named Bimbisara.

00:13:55.590 --> 00:14:01.730
He sent a gift to ta kob kyi gyelpo (MTHA' 'KHOB KYI RGYAL PO)
means "to a king who was living in the

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:04.759
outlying parts of India," not the center of India.

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:15.410
That king's name was Udrayana--sometimes they say with Utriana--

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:24.470
and Udrayana had sent a very valuable gift to Bimbisara.

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:31.399
Udrayana first sent a very valuable gift to Bimbisara.

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:38.360
As I remember, it was a special suit of armor.

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:49.939
And then Bimbisara couldn't decide on something more expensive to send back.

00:14:56.670 --> 00:14:59.240
So it ended up he asked Lord Buddha,

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:07.699
and Lord Buddha directed the painting of the Wheel of Life.

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:15.540
And

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:19.399
I've been studying that for many years and

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:24.980
I think we translated ... we made a book about it thirty years ago,

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:34.639
and we illustrated the book with some line drawings that we made

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:49.490
based on the ancient stone carvings in Borobudur, in Indonesia.

00:15:58.589 --> 00:16:00.589
And you can see here an image,

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:09.290
there's a elephant and on top of the elephant there's a guy, and the guy has a box in his hands,

00:16:13.889 --> 00:16:18.529
and he's delivering this gift from Bimbisara to King Udrayana.

00:16:24.569 --> 00:16:28.159
And we translated that story.

00:16:30.700 --> 00:16:32.880
It's called "King Udrayana and the Wheel of Life.

00:16:38.660 --> 00:16:41.520
kyo she kyepay rab (SKYO SHAS SKYES PA'I RABS) means
there's a

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:44.920
recorded history

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:51.769
of how Udrayana, when he saw the painting of the twelve links,

00:16:57.600 --> 00:17:02.540
he got kyo she (SKYO SHAS). kyo she means "Ah, this life is so much suffering."

00:17:07.709 --> 00:17:09.709
So Pabongka Rinpoche says,

00:17:12.120 --> 00:17:19.339
"Let's follow those same twelve links and see if we can get the same kind of feeling about 'Ah man, this life is all suffering.'"

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:35.029
Then we will want to get out. And that's the goal of this section of the lam rim.

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:47.390
He also says:
geshe puchungway tendrel la lojong dze ching diy go ne kye bu chung dring la tri tsul sung
(DGE BSHES PHU CHUNG BAS RTEN 'BREL LA BLO SBYONG MDZAD CING 'DI'I SGO NAS SKYES BU CHUNG 'BRING LA 'KHRID TSUL GSUNGS)--

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:50.869
Pabongka Rinpoche, in his lecture,

00:17:53.820 --> 00:18:00.919
also described how we could develop these attitudes based on a teaching by Geshe Puchungwa.

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:13.010
This geshe lived a thousand years ago

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:18.920
and he wrote a book about dependence,

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:25.790
and so we're going to move on to this teaching of dependence.

00:18:29.550 --> 00:18:32.539
Here you can see the whole Wheel of Life painting

00:18:36.180 --> 00:18:38.629
that was presented to King Udrayana

00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:42.890
by King Bimbisara.

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:49.700
I've studied... there are a few other sutras where Buddha sends this painting to people.

00:18:57.420 --> 00:19:03.769
Sometimes he sends it to people who would not be able to read a sutra if he sent them a sutra,

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:13.250
and those are beautiful stories that you should study sometime.

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:20.479
Now there's a difference between the Wheel of Life and the twelve links.

00:19:26.370 --> 00:19:30.829
The Wheel of Life includes the teaching on the twelve links, and here you see a

00:19:31.559 --> 00:19:35.239
separation of the isolation of the twelve links--the circle with the twelve links.

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:52.640
So, technically that teaching on these twelve links was presented separately from the Wheel, also.

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:02.270
We're going to go back to the text now.

00:20:04.409 --> 00:20:11.299
dir yang kyowa shuk drakpa sok kyi chir
('DIR YANG SKYO BA SHUGS DRAG PA SOGS KYI PHYIR)--
So to try to give us the same feeling of

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:14.750
being discouraged with this life.

00:20:21.960 --> 00:20:26.870
tru ne (SPROS NAS)--we're going to start with that as a goal,
and we're going to

00:20:27.419 --> 00:20:31.309
go into a new subject with that goal.

00:20:38.420 --> 00:20:42.580
We're going to use tendrel yanlak chu nyi kyi go ne 
(RTEN 'BREL YAN LAG BCU GNYIS KYI SGO NAS)--we are going to use the twelve links

00:20:45.740 --> 00:20:49.200
kun jung korway nye mik sampa te
(KUN 'BYUNG 'KHOR BA'I NYES DMIGS BSAM PA STE)--

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:53.959
to do our new contemplation about how terrible this life is 
and how we got into this trouble.

00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:12.469
Now these twelve links were... one of the great sources for these twelve links is a teaching by Lord Buddha.

00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:24.349
In Tibetan, it is called sa lu jangpay do (SA LU LJANG PA'I MDO), 
which you see here.

00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:34.130
And in Sanskrit, that's Shalistambha Sutra,

00:21:36.270 --> 00:21:40.280
which means "The Sutra about a Sprout of Rice."

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:50.749
He says, "That's really where this idea of the twelve links started out. You should read that."

00:21:57.840 --> 00:22:00.020
Then he quotes that sutra briefly:

00:22:02.790 --> 00:22:07.519
de yang, di yupay di jung, di kye pay di kyewa
(DE YANG, 'DI YOD PAS 'DI 'BYUNG, 'DI SKYES PAS 'DI SKYE BA). Okay, very famous.

00:22:09.390 --> 00:22:14.359
In that sutra, Lord Buddha starts the discussion

00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:23.240
by saying, "If this is here, then this is going to be here, then this is going to happen.

00:22:26.450 --> 00:22:29.539
And if this thing starts, then this thing is going to start."

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:36.109
"And the whole trouble starts with misunderstanding the world,

00:22:41.240 --> 00:22:43.460
and then we start to make karma."

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:46.780
Et cetera, et cetera.

00:22:47.639 --> 00:22:50.809
He doesn't say more from the sutra.

00:22:52.739 --> 00:22:54.739
But I really like that sutra,

00:22:56.309 --> 00:23:02.599
so I'm going to inject a little bit of the sutra in today's session.

00:23:08.159 --> 00:23:10.518
It's going to make the session a little longer

00:23:12.539 --> 00:23:14.539
and I don't care.

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:16.730
So just be patient,

00:23:16.950 --> 00:23:18.950
and this is fun,

00:23:19.379 --> 00:23:21.379
and we're going to go deeper,

00:23:21.809 --> 00:23:25.128
that's the way we're doing the Steps on the Path.

00:23:27.929 --> 00:23:33.559
I like the first paragraph or two of that sutra and

00:23:35.789 --> 00:23:37.939
I've included it in the reading for you.

00:23:42.899 --> 00:23:47.149
di la chomden de chukyi dakpa tamche kenpay
('DI LA BCOM LDAN 'DAS CHOS KYI BDAGS PO THAMS CAD MKHYEN PAS)--

00:23:48.210 --> 00:23:50.689
Now, because the Buddha knows

00:23:51.539 --> 00:23:53.039
everything

00:23:53.039 --> 00:23:55.039
that there is in the whole universe,

00:24:01.769 --> 00:24:04.969
he said to the monks who came to the teaching,

00:24:09.419 --> 00:24:11.419
gelong dak (DGE SLONG DAG)--"Hey, you monks!"

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:16.129
su ten ching drelwar jungwa tongwa de chu tong go
(SUS RTEN CING 'BREL BAR 'BYUNG BA MTHONG BA DES CHOS MTHONG NGO)--

00:24:16.710 --> 00:24:20.299
"Anyone who sees that things happen

00:24:20.879 --> 00:24:22.879
in dependence on each other,

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:32.449
the person who understands dependence understands my Dharma."

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:37.860
"And

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:41.479
anyone who understands.." sorry, let's put it this way--

00:24:42.659 --> 00:24:46.939
"Any person who sees that things work in dependence on each other,

00:24:52.830 --> 00:24:54.830
then that person sees the real Dharma. 

00:24:57.929 --> 00:25:00.288
And if a person sees that real Dharma"--

00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:06.020
dependence--"then they saw the Buddha."

00:25:09.270 --> 00:25:16.280
So who said that? The Buddha. Okay? "If you see how things depend on each other,

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:22.940
then you saw my true Dharma.

00:25:24.450 --> 00:25:26.450
And if you see my true Dharma

00:25:28.169 --> 00:25:30.169
then you actually saw me."

00:25:32.309 --> 00:25:39.319
ten ching drelwar jungwa gang she na
(RTEN CING 'BREL BAR 'BYUNG BA GANG ZHE NA)--
So everyone... of course the monks all say, "Oh, what's this dependence thing? We want to see, we want to see!"

00:25:44.850 --> 00:25:49.909
ten chin drelwar jungwa gang she na--"But what is dependence?" 
Who's talking?

00:25:51.510 --> 00:25:55.729
Buddha.  
ten chin drelwar jungwa she jawa ni
(RTEN CING 'BREL BAR 'BYUNG BA ZHES BYA BA NI)--
"You want to know what dependence is?"

00:25:57.240 --> 00:25:59.240
di ta te ('DI LTA STE)--"Well, here's how it is."

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:04.730
di yupay di jung ('DI YOD PAS 'DI 'BYUNG)--"If this happens, this happens."

00:26:08.159 --> 00:26:12.229
de kyepay chir di kye ('DI SKYES PA'I PHYIR 'DI SKYE)--
"And if this starts, than that is going to start."

00:26:14.549 --> 00:26:17.059
Okay, and then they are like, "What do you mean?"

00:26:20.340 --> 00:26:22.730
"Look, if you don't understand the world--

00:26:27.679 --> 00:26:29.679
if you misunderstand the world--

00:26:31.100 --> 00:26:38.740
then you're going to make karma. 
If you make karma, you make awareness.

00:26:40.220 --> 00:26:44.230
If you make awareness, you make body and mind.

00:26:45.500 --> 00:26:50.619
If you make body and mind, 
you make the six doors of sense.

00:26:52.190 --> 00:26:54.520
If you have the six doors of sense,

00:26:55.880 --> 00:26:57.880
you make contact with the outside world.

00:27:01.309 --> 00:27:03.309
If you make contact with the outside world,

00:27:05.750 --> 00:27:07.750
then you're going to feel feelings.

00:27:09.500 --> 00:27:11.500
If you feel feelings,

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:14.830
then desire is going to start.

00:27:15.740 --> 00:27:17.740
If desire starts,

00:27:18.890 --> 00:27:20.890
desire is going to grow.

00:27:21.470 --> 00:27:23.470
If desire grows,

00:27:24.590 --> 00:27:26.590
you will be born.

00:27:27.799 --> 00:27:32.109
And if you are born,
you must get old and you must die.

00:27:34.159 --> 00:27:37.869
And you will suffer,

00:27:39.230 --> 00:27:41.230
you will cry out in pain,

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:46.000
more and more pain in your life,

00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:50.229
and more and more sadness in your heart,

00:27:51.950 --> 00:27:54.340
and then truly chaos.

00:27:57.650 --> 00:28:01.539
This whole life is a mountain of pain."

00:28:04.460 --> 00:28:10.809
Then I think everybody's like... you can feel the monks are sitting there going...

00:28:13.820 --> 00:28:18.970
And I think automatically everyone in the room, or everyone in the field,

00:28:22.220 --> 00:28:25.419
they think, "Well, can we do anything about it?"

00:28:27.920 --> 00:28:30.340
Buddha is like ... "No problem."

00:28:33.170 --> 00:28:38.259
ma rikpa gakpay duje gak (MA RIG PA 'GAGS PAS 'DU BYED 'GAG)--
"If you stop misunderstanding your world, 
you won't make this bad karma anymore.

00:28:43.130 --> 00:28:47.440
If you don't make karma anymore,
you don't have this negative awareness that we have.

00:28:53.690 --> 00:28:59.950
If you don't have that kind of awareness, 
you're not going to pick up a painful body and mind inside your mom.

00:29:09.740 --> 00:29:15.850
If you stop that mortal body and mind inside your mom, 
you're not going to have the sense organs.

00:29:25.610 --> 00:29:27.610
Those painful sense organs.

00:29:28.010 --> 00:29:30.010
If you can stop them,

00:29:31.370 --> 00:29:34.060
then you don't make painful contact with the world.

00:29:37.160 --> 00:29:40.509
If you stop painful contact with the world,

00:29:45.320 --> 00:29:47.470
then you stop painful feelings.

00:29:50.810 --> 00:29:57.369
If you stop those feelings, 
you will stop the beginning of desire.

00:30:00.200 --> 00:30:05.649
If you stop the beginning of desire, 
you will stop the more deep kinds of desire.

00:30:10.010 --> 00:30:16.449
If you stop that advanced desire, 
then you won't trigger the karma at death," which we've been talking about.

00:30:26.420 --> 00:30:28.420
By the way, I think I forgot to mention that

00:30:29.030 --> 00:30:35.950
link before, just now ... I didn't mention that one, but your karma is triggered.

00:30:42.050 --> 00:30:44.260
"If you stop how the karma is triggered,

00:30:46.850 --> 00:30:50.079
then you can stop having these deadly rebirths.

00:30:54.050 --> 00:30:56.440
If you stop these deadly rebirths,

00:30:59.540 --> 00:31:02.440
then no more aging and death, in the old way.

00:31:06.050 --> 00:31:10.690
No more sadness. No more crying out in pain.

00:31:13.070 --> 00:31:15.070
No more physical pain.

00:31:16.760 --> 00:31:23.109
No more mental pain. And no more of the chaos of this life.

00:31:24.470 --> 00:31:28.510
And that's how you stop this big mountain of suffering."

00:31:31.610 --> 00:31:39.189
And this concludes the statement, the first big statement, by Lord Buddha of the twelve links.

00:31:44.660 --> 00:31:46.660
Now last thing before we stop ...

00:31:50.120 --> 00:31:52.460
there are a couple of things I'd like to share with you.

00:31:54.260 --> 00:31:56.260
So, you know we have a

00:31:58.100 --> 00:32:01.299
group of translators, they are translating the ancient books.

00:32:05.780 --> 00:32:13.780
We chose eighteen of the most important great ancient classics from each of the great schools.

00:32:22.310 --> 00:32:29.889
And one of the books we are translating--and the translator is Sugeng Shi, who is also from Indonesia and Singapore--

00:32:35.990 --> 00:32:42.160
he's translating a very deep, very detailed, very long book about these twelve links.

00:32:48.170 --> 00:32:53.499
It was written, I checked, in 1550 ... around 1550,

00:32:54.890 --> 00:32:56.660
and

00:32:56.660 --> 00:32:59.920
I checked to see what was happening in the Western countries at that time.

00:33:03.770 --> 00:33:06.580
They estimate this was the year that Shakespeare was born,

00:33:13.460 --> 00:33:18.279
and this is the year that Michelangelo did some famous paintings in Italy.

00:33:23.180 --> 00:33:27.219
So we're about to go into the twelve links from the Wheel of Life,

00:33:32.450 --> 00:33:37.119
but if you're a very ambitious student who wants to go deep, deep, deep,

00:33:42.500 --> 00:33:45.040
then I strongly suggest you go read

00:33:45.560 --> 00:33:50.979
the translation that Sugeng has finished so far. 
He's not finished with the whole book, but he's almost finished.

00:34:02.470 --> 00:34:06.750
We're going to put up here for you the link to that website.

00:34:12.460 --> 00:34:15.480
All those eighteen translations that we are working on,

00:34:18.520 --> 00:34:22.080
the greatest books of the greatest schools of the last two and a half thousand years,

00:34:30.490 --> 00:34:34.050
you can go read them for free on the website.

00:34:38.649 --> 00:34:45.598
We call our little group, the unofficial official name is "Mixed Nuts,"

00:34:51.940 --> 00:34:58.230
because we're all a little bit crazy and we come from a whole mixture of different cultures in the world.

00:35:03.180 --> 00:35:09.200
So, if you really want to go deeper into this subject, 

00:35:11.220 --> 00:35:13.220
I strongly suggest you go read

00:35:14.200 --> 00:35:17.460
Sugeng's translation, which is available for free on the website.

00:35:24.070 --> 00:35:32.009
Now, if you get excited about that, and someday you want to learn translation yourself,

00:35:35.770 --> 00:35:39.389
there are 300,000 books, not eighteen! We need help.

00:35:44.650 --> 00:35:48.060
Then you should start listening to those translation classes.

00:35:50.830 --> 00:35:58.379
Are they difficult? Yes, they are difficult. Will you get confused at the beginning? Yes, you will get confused at the beginning.

00:36:02.410 --> 00:36:07.139
Will you be like Master Xuanzang, back in the Tang Dynasty, who

00:36:07.839 --> 00:36:12.129
started a whole tradition for a billion people?

00:36:22.159 --> 00:36:24.159
Yeah, you can help.

00:36:25.489 --> 00:36:30.999
There are six hundred free translation classes online.

00:36:36.289 --> 00:36:40.839
Go to YouTube; it's called Tibetan Language Channel.

00:36:46.099 --> 00:36:48.639
Maybe if you're very interested in the twelve links,

00:36:52.309 --> 00:36:55.508
you can follow the classes that Sugeng had,

00:37:00.259 --> 00:37:08.109
which is almost a hundred classes. Now, if you're very ambitious--

00:37:10.640 --> 00:37:13.329
like you really want to go deep, deep, deep, deep, deep--

00:37:15.169 --> 00:37:18.009
I think those classes--

00:37:20.150 --> 00:37:24.279
which are being held in this building, in the Sedona College of International

00:37:24.919 --> 00:37:27.489
Management, in Sedona, Arizona,

00:37:28.459 --> 00:37:30.459
USA--

00:37:38.509 --> 00:37:44.198
We are covering every major school of twenty-five centuries of Buddhism,

00:37:52.350 --> 00:37:55.279
and I'm putting up here an email address,

00:37:58.950 --> 00:38:04.130
of Nick Lashaw, who's the director of the program.

00:38:07.080 --> 00:38:10.340
Those programs are held three times a year for two weeks.

00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:22.340
I think truly it's one of the big moments in history.

00:38:27.000 --> 00:38:31.250
And to me, we would love to have you come.

00:38:34.770 --> 00:38:37.190
You start as an observer in the class,

00:38:40.350 --> 00:38:42.650
and then slowly you become a translator.

00:38:43.980 --> 00:38:46.790
Here's a picture of one of the classes,

00:38:48.720 --> 00:38:50.990
and I would love to see you there.

00:38:53.700 --> 00:38:55.700
You are very,

00:38:55.800 --> 00:38:57.800
very welcome to come and

00:38:58.950 --> 00:39:02.180
I believe it's one of the most important things in this world.

00:39:06.150 --> 00:39:08.150
So that's session ten.

00:39:09.960 --> 00:39:14.150
Thanks to Stanley. Thanks to Tim for getting it all organized.

00:39:15.690 --> 00:39:19.280
And we're about to step into the Wheel of Life.

00:39:21.450 --> 00:39:23.450
We'll see you then.

00:39:23.910 --> 00:39:25.910
Session eleven.

